---
import Layout from "../layouts/layout.astro";

// Client agreement, opened by unique link: /web-contract?t=<token>. The token is
// looked up against D1 (worker/index.ts) to learn who it was minted for and
// whether it has already been signed. Everything below the fold is the agreement
// itself, which lives here in git rather than in the database, so it is diffable.
//
// noindex because a signing link is not a public page. It is unguessable rather
// than authenticated, which is the right bar for a two-party services agreement
// and the wrong bar for anything with real secrets in it.
//
// ponytail: one agreement body, because there is one client. A second contract
// shape means a second slug and a second page, not a templating layer.
---

<Layout
	title="Services Agreement - Zabaca"
	description="Client services agreement."
	noindex
>
	<main class="contract-page">
		<div class="contract-shell">
			<p class="state" id="state">Loading agreement...</p>

			<article id="doc" hidden>
				<header class="doc-head">
					<p class="eyebrow">Zabaca LLC</p>
					<h1>Website Services Agreement</h1>
					<p class="for-line">Prepared for <strong id="client-name"></strong></p>
				</header>

				<!-- Everything inside #agreement is fingerprinted (SHA-256 of its
				     normalized text) and stored with the signature. Editing any word in
				     here after someone signs makes the stored hash stop matching, which
				     is the point: it is what stops the page quietly becoming a different
				     agreement than the one that was accepted. -->
				<section id="agreement">
					<p>
						<strong>Between:</strong> Zabaca LLC, a California limited liability
						company ("Zabaca"), and Lee &amp; Company Construction Holdings Inc., a
						California corporation, CSLB license #1038450, of 411 E Huntington Dr.,
						Ste 107-322, Arcadia, CA 91006 ("Lee &amp; Co").
					</p>

					<h2>1. What Zabaca builds and launches</h2>
					<p>
						A replacement website for leeandcoconstruction.com, moved off Wix. The
						launch includes:
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							The new site, built and hosted by Zabaca, carrying over Lee &amp; Co's
							existing content plus a guided project estimate form.
						</li>
						<li>
							Migration of the domain leeandcoconstruction.com off Wix, with the
							name staying registered to Lee &amp; Co.
						</li>
						<li>
							Lead notifications. Every estimate request is delivered to Lee &amp; Co
							by email, text message, or Telegram, whichever Lee &amp; Co prefers.
						</li>
						<li>
							Reconnection of Lee &amp; Co's existing Google Analytics and Google Ads
							accounts after the move, so historical data and any running ads keep
							working.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						There is no separate build fee. The build is covered by the monthly fee
						in section 4.
					</p>

					<h2>2. What Lee &amp; Co provides</h2>
					<p>Zabaca needs all of the following to do the work in section 1:</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Access to the Wix account, the domain registrar, Google Analytics, and
							Google Ads.
						</li>
						<li>
							Review time. Zabaca walks through each screen and Lee &amp; Co responds
							with changes.
						</li>
						<li>
							Photographs of Lee &amp; Co's own completed projects. The site currently
							uses stock photography, and real project photos are the single largest
							improvement available to it.
						</li>
						<li>
							Confirmation of whether any email address runs through Wix or through
							the domain. If one does, it must be identified before the domain is
							moved.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						Lee &amp; Co keeps its Wix subscription active until the new site is live
						and confirmed working. Cancelling Wix early can take the existing site,
						and any Wix-hosted email, offline.
					</p>

					<h2>3. Launch</h2>
					<p>
						Zabaca walks through the site with Lee &amp; Co, screen by screen, and
						revisions continue until Lee &amp; Co approves it for launch. How quickly
						that happens depends mostly on how quickly Lee &amp; Co can review and
						respond.
					</p>

					<h2>4. Fee</h2>
					<p>
						<strong>$200 per month</strong>, beginning the month this agreement is
						signed. It covers:
					</p>
					<ul>
						<li>
							Hosting, SSL certificate, domain management, and keeping the site
							online.
						</li>
						<li>
							Up to two change requests per month, such as text edits, swapping
							photographs, or adding or updating a page. Unused requests do not carry
							over.
						</li>
					</ul>
					<p>
						Work beyond that, including new features, new sections of the site, and
						management of ad campaigns, is quoted and agreed separately before it
						starts.
					</p>

					<h2>5. Product feedback</h2>
					<p>
						The $200 rate reflects that Lee &amp; Co is a design partner, not only a
						client. Lee &amp; Co agrees to make Chris Lee available for up to two hours
						per month to review and give feedback on software Zabaca is developing for
						the construction trade.
					</p>
					<p>
						Zabaca owns everything it builds, including work informed by that
						feedback. Lee &amp; Co receives no ownership, equity, or royalty in it, and
						owes Zabaca nothing for it.
					</p>
					<p>
						If Lee &amp; Co stops participating, Zabaca may change the monthly fee on
						30 days' written notice. Lee &amp; Co may end this agreement instead of
						accepting a new fee.
					</p>

					<h2>6. Who owns what</h2>
					<ul>
						<li>
							<strong>Lee &amp; Co owns</strong> the domain name, its business name and
							logo, all text and photographs it supplies, and every lead and customer
							detail the site collects.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>Zabaca owns</strong> the site's code and the underlying tooling,
							and licenses it to Lee &amp; Co for use on leeandcoconstruction.com for
							as long as this agreement is in effect.
						</li>
						<li>
							<strong>When this agreement ends, for any reason,</strong> Zabaca provides
							a complete copy of the finished website as standard web files that any
							hosting company can serve, and transfers full control of the domain to
							Lee &amp; Co. Lee &amp; Co is not left without a website.
						</li>
					</ul>

					<h2>7. What is not promised</h2>
					<p>
						Zabaca does not promise any search ranking, level of traffic, or number of
						leads. Those outcomes depend on factors outside Zabaca's control.
					</p>

					<h2>8. Term, ending it, and limits</h2>
					<p>
						This agreement runs month to month. There is no minimum term.
					</p>
					<p>
						Either side may end it with 30 days' written notice, or immediately if the
						other side materially breaches it and does not fix the breach within 15
						days of being told about it in writing.
					</p>
					<p>
						Zabaca's total liability under this agreement is limited to the fees Lee
						&amp; Co paid in the 12 months before the claim. Neither side is liable to
						the other for lost profits or other indirect losses. This agreement is
						governed by the laws of the State of California.
					</p>
				</section>

				<section class="signatures">
					<h2>Signatures</h2>

					<div class="sig-block signed">
						<p class="sig-party">Zabaca LLC</p>
						<p class="sig-name">James Lee, Member</p>
						<p class="sig-meta">Signed on issue of this agreement.</p>
					</div>

					<div class="sig-block" id="client-sig">
						<p class="sig-party">Lee &amp; Company Construction Holdings Inc.</p>

						<div id="sign-form">
							<label for="signer-name">Full legal name</label>
							<input
								id="signer-name"
								type="text"
								autocomplete="name"
								placeholder="Chris Lee"
							/>

							<label for="signer-title">Title</label>
							<input
								id="signer-title"
								type="text"
								autocomplete="organization-title"
								placeholder="Owner"
							/>

							<label class="agree-line">
								<input id="agree" type="checkbox" />
								<span>
									I have read this agreement and I agree to it on behalf of Lee &amp;
									Company Construction Holdings Inc. I intend this to be my electronic
									signature.
								</span>
							</label>

							<button id="sign-btn" type="button" disabled>Sign agreement</button>
							<p class="err" id="err" hidden></p>
						</div>

						<div id="signed-state" hidden>
							<p class="sig-name" id="signed-name"></p>
							<p class="sig-meta" id="signed-meta"></p>
						</div>
					</div>

					<p class="retain">
						This page stays at this link after signing. Print or save it for your
						records.
					</p>
				</section>
			</article>
		</div>
	</main>
</Layout>

<script>
	const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
	const token = params.get("t");

	const el = (id: string) => document.getElementById(id) as HTMLElement;
	const stateEl = el("state");
	const doc = el("doc");

	function fail(message: string) {
		stateEl.textContent = message;
		stateEl.hidden = false;
		doc.hidden = true;
	}

	/**
	 * Fingerprint of the agreement as rendered. Whitespace is collapsed so that
	 * reformatting the markup (which changes indentation, not meaning) does not
	 * invalidate a signature, while any change to the words does.
	 */
	async function agreementHash(): Promise<string> {
		const text = (el("agreement").textContent ?? "").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
		const digest = await crypto.subtle.digest(
			"SHA-256",
			new TextEncoder().encode(text),
		);
		return [...new Uint8Array(digest)]
			.map((b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0"))
			.join("");
	}

	function renderSigned(signed: { at: string; name: string; title: string | null }) {
		el("sign-form").hidden = true;
		el("signed-state").hidden = false;
		el("signed-name").textContent = signed.title
			? `${signed.name}, ${signed.title}`
			: signed.name;
		el("signed-meta").textContent = `Signed ${new Date(signed.at).toLocaleString()}`;
		el("client-sig").classList.add("signed");
	}

	async function load() {
		if (!token) return fail("This signing link is not valid.");
		const res = await fetch(`/api/contract?t=${encodeURIComponent(token)}`);
		if (!res.ok) return fail("This signing link is not valid.");
		const data = await res.json();

		el("client-name").textContent = data.clientEntity ?? data.clientName;
		stateEl.hidden = true;
		doc.hidden = false;
		if (data.signed) renderSigned(data.signed);
	}

	const agree = el("agree") as HTMLInputElement;
	const nameInput = el("signer-name") as HTMLInputElement;
	const btn = el("sign-btn") as HTMLButtonElement;

	function syncButton() {
		btn.disabled = !(agree.checked && nameInput.value.trim().length >= 2);
	}
	agree.addEventListener("change", syncButton);
	nameInput.addEventListener("input", syncButton);

	btn.addEventListener("click", async () => {
		btn.disabled = true;
		const errEl = el("err");
		errEl.hidden = true;
		const res = await fetch("/api/contract/sign", {
			method: "POST",
			headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
			body: JSON.stringify({
				t: token,
				name: nameInput.value,
				title: (el("signer-title") as HTMLInputElement).value,
				agreementSha256: await agreementHash(),
			}),
		});
		const data = await res.json();
		// 409 means someone already signed it, which is a state to render, not an error.
		if (res.ok || data.alreadySigned) return renderSigned(data.signed);
		errEl.textContent = data.error ?? "Could not record the signature.";
		errEl.hidden = false;
		syncButton();
	});

	load();
</script>

<style>
	.contract-page {
		background: #f7f7f5;
		min-height: 100vh;
		padding: 3rem 1.25rem 5rem;
	}
	.contract-shell {
		max-width: 46rem;
		margin: 0 auto;
		background: #fff;
		border: 1px solid #e3e3df;
		border-radius: 4px;
		padding: 2.5rem 2rem 3rem;
	}
	.state {
		color: #6b6b66;
		text-align: center;
		padding: 3rem 0;
	}
	.doc-head {
		border-bottom: 2px solid #131416;
		padding-bottom: 1.25rem;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}
	.eyebrow {
		text-transform: uppercase;
		letter-spacing: 0.12em;
		font-size: 0.75rem;
		color: #6b6b66;
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
	}
	.doc-head h1 {
		font-size: 1.75rem;
		line-height: 1.2;
		margin: 0 0 0.5rem;
	}
	.for-line {
		margin: 0;
		color: #46464a;
	}
	#agreement h2 {
		font-size: 1.05rem;
		margin: 2rem 0 0.6rem;
	}
	#agreement p,
	#agreement li {
		line-height: 1.6;
		color: #26262a;
	}
	/* Both of these fight global.css, which zeroes paragraph margins and strips
	   list markers for the marketing pages. A contract needs its clauses visually
	   separated and its lists to read as lists. */
	#agreement p {
		margin: 0 0 0.85rem;
	}
	#agreement ul {
		list-style: disc;
		padding-left: 1.35rem;
		margin: 0 0 1rem;
	}
	#agreement li {
		margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
	}
	.signatures {
		margin-top: 3rem;
		border-top: 2px solid #131416;
		padding-top: 1.5rem;
	}
	.sig-block {
		border: 1px solid #e3e3df;
		border-radius: 4px;
		padding: 1.25rem;
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
	}
	.sig-block.signed {
		border-color: #b9d8c0;
		background: #f4faf5;
	}
	.sig-party {
		font-weight: 700;
		margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
	}
	.sig-name {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 1.05rem;
	}
	.sig-meta {
		margin: 0.25rem 0 0;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: #6b6b66;
	}
	label {
		display: block;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		margin: 0.9rem 0 0.3rem;
	}
	input[type="text"] {
		width: 100%;
		padding: 0.6rem 0.7rem;
		border: 1px solid #c9c9c3;
		border-radius: 3px;
		font-size: 1rem;
	}
	.agree-line {
		display: flex;
		gap: 0.6rem;
		align-items: flex-start;
		font-weight: 400;
		line-height: 1.5;
		margin-top: 1.1rem;
	}
	.agree-line input {
		margin-top: 0.25rem;
		flex-shrink: 0;
	}
	#sign-btn {
		margin-top: 1.25rem;
		width: 100%;
		padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
		font-size: 1rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		border: 0;
		border-radius: 3px;
		background: #131416;
		color: #fff;
		cursor: pointer;
	}
	#sign-btn:disabled {
		background: #c9c9c3;
		cursor: not-allowed;
	}
	.err {
		color: #a3232b;
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		margin-top: 0.75rem;
	}
	.retain {
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: #6b6b66;
		margin-top: 1.5rem;
	}
	@media (max-width: 640px) {
		.contract-page {
			padding: 1.5rem 0.75rem 3rem;
		}
		.contract-shell {
			padding: 1.5rem 1.1rem 2rem;
		}
	}
</style>
